            <ArticleTitle>Coronary-Cameral Fistula Caused by Guidewire Trauma and Resolved by Coil Embolization.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>338-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.14503/THIJ-15-5177</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 57-year-old woman presented with effort angina. A coronary angiogram revealed critical 2-vessel disease, for which she subsequently underwent percutaneous coronary intervention. During angioplasty, a coronary guidewire-inadvertently passed into the right ventricle through the septal branches of the posterior descending coronary artery-caused a coronary artery-to-right ventricular fistula. This fistula was successfully closed percutaneously by coil embolization. To our knowledge, this is the first report of a case in which a coronary artery-to-right ventricular fistula caused by a guidewire was managed successfully by coil embolization.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
